---
/**Story: 5 Biggest Budget Shifts in 2026.*/
import Story from '../../layouts/Story.astro';
import ChangesChart from '../../components/ChangesChart.tsx';
import budgetChanges from '../../data/views/budget-changes.json';

const data = budgetChanges as any;
const topInc = data.topIncreases.slice(0, 5);
const topDec = data.topDecreases.slice(0, 5);
const totalChange = data.totalBudget2026 - data.totalBudget2025;
const changePct = ((totalChange / data.totalBudget2025) * 100).toFixed(1);

function fmtM(n: number): string {
  return `$${(Math.abs(n) / 1_000_000).toFixed(1)}M`;
}
---

<Story
  title="5 Biggest Budget Shifts in 2026"
  subtitle="Where the money moved — and what it tells us about city priorities"
  description="The top budget increases and decreases in Pittsburgh's FY2026 operating budget, and what they mean."
  date="March 2026"
>
  <p>
    Pittsburgh's operating budget grew by <strong>{changePct}%</strong> this year, from
    {fmtM(data.totalBudget2025)} to {fmtM(data.totalBudget2026)}. But the headline number
    masks significant shifts underneath — some departments saw double-digit increases while
    others were cut. Here are the five biggest moves.
  </p>

  <h2>The Biggest Increases</h2>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <ChangesChart data={topInc} title="" color="#16a34a" client:load />
  </div>

  <h3>1. Bureau of Administration (Public Works): +{fmtM(topInc[0]?.dollarChange)} (+{topInc[0]?.pctChange}%)</h3>
  <p>
    The single largest dollar increase in the budget. Public Works Administration handles
    the back-office operations that keep streets paved, trash collected, and infrastructure
    maintained. An 80% jump signals major operational expansion or consolidation of functions
    from other bureaus.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Bureau of Emergency Medical Services: +{fmtM(topInc[1]?.dollarChange)} (+{topInc[1]?.pctChange}%)</h3>
  <p>
    EMS sees a 41% increase — one of the largest percentage jumps among major departments.
    This likely reflects rising costs for paramedic staffing, ambulance fleet maintenance,
    and the ongoing challenge of competitive pay in a tight labor market for first responders.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Bureau of Fire: +{fmtM(topInc[2]?.dollarChange)} (+{topInc[2]?.pctChange}%)</h3>
  <p>
    With 679 FTE positions, Fire is one of the city's largest departments. An 8% increase
    on a base of nearly $100M means close to $8M in additional spending — largely driven by
    contractual salary increases and rising benefit costs.
  </p>

  <h2>The Biggest Decreases</h2>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <ChangesChart data={topDec} title="" color="#dc2626" client:load />
  </div>

  <h3>4. Liquid Fuels Trust Fund: {fmtM(topDec[0]?.dollarChange)} ({topDec[0]?.pctChange}%)</h3>
  <p>
    The largest dollar decrease. This state-funded trust for road maintenance dropped by over 20%.
    This could reflect changes in state gasoline tax allocations or a shift in how infrastructure
    spending is categorized between operating and capital budgets.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Department of Parks and Recreation: {fmtM(topDec[1]?.dollarChange)} ({topDec[1]?.pctChange}%)</h3>
  <p>
    Parks sees a modest 2.2% cut despite the city's stated commitment to green space.
    The reduction may reflect one-time spending from 2025 that doesn't recur, or a shift
    of capital-eligible projects from the operating budget to the capital plan.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>The staffing picture:</strong> Across the city, {data.newPositions.length} positions are
      being created and {data.eliminatedPositions.length} eliminated — a net change of
      {data.totalFte2026 - data.totalFte2025 > 0 ? '+' : ''}{Math.round(data.totalFte2026 - data.totalFte2025)} FTE.
      See the full breakdown on the <a href="/changes/" class="underline">What Changed</a> page.
    </p>
  </div>
</Story>
